---
// The front door. The original spec had the hero *be* the live
// story rather than describe it; this page is the marketing alternative
// the user asked for instead — hero, features, one CTA into the app. The
// spec's own `/` still stands at /today. Copy follows product/features.md's tone:
// plain, specific, warm, proportional praise, no urgency or scarcity.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import "../styles/landing.css";

// "Get started" is the explicit entry into onboarding, so it clears the
// "already done this" flag first — a device that finished onboarding
// once (in testing, or a returning visitor bookmarking /today directly)
// still gets it again from here. Key has to match mount-onboarding.js's
// KEY constant.
---

<Base title="Hope By Bit — one good thing, one small thing, every day">
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>One good thing. One small thing. Every day.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A genuinely good piece of news, matched to what you care about. One small thing your body can
      do about it. And a record of it all, quietly adding up in your corner.
    </p>
    <a class="btn" href="/today" onclick="localStorage.removeItem('hopebybit:onboarded')">Get started</a>
  </section>

  <section class="features">
    <article class="feature">
      <h2>One story a day</h2>
      <p>
        Matched to three to five things you pick — medicine, climate, wild places, kindness,
        science, your city, rights, food. One story, then the screen ends. You're done, and
        that's the point.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="feature">
      <h2>Something your body can do</h2>
      <p>
        A glass of water. Stand up and stretch. Ninety seconds outside. Nothing costs anything,
        nothing needs a signup, and none of it asks you to wait for a better week.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="feature">
      <h2>Keep the ones that matter</h2>
      <p>
        The wall is everything you did and kept, newest first — a total that only grows. On a bad
        day it's proof you were here for it, and it belongs to you.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="feature">
      <h2>Break the loop</h2>
      <p>
        For your head, alone, open as often as the day needs. Sixty seconds: a breath, one of
        your own kept stories, one sensory question. It costs nothing and never leaves your phone.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="feature">
      <h2>Check in</h2>
      <p>
        For your people. Two or three chosen in advance, each one agreeing first. One tap sends
        the message you wrote while it was calm — so there's nothing to find the words for now.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</Base>
